hi everyone I'm Anthony Silvano CBS News  elections and surveys directors and  director enjoining me in the Toyota  greenroom is Ashley Edwards refinery29 s  senior news and politics editor we have  teamed up here at CBS News with  refinery29 to explore the impact of  millennial women on the coming midterm  elections Ashley good to see you so we  just finished this poll we just put out  the releases and one of the things that  I think struck everybody was the turnout  question and as we head towards midterms  everybody's always asking matters the  most  yeah the turnout questions visit depends  who shows up right so so millennial  women many of them said that they were  excited enthusiastic about voting but  they were less likely compared to older  women compared to the older generation  what did you make of that and why might  that be I think people are energized  they're angry however that doesn't  necessarily translate into actually  going out to the voting booth they may  not be excited about the candidates that  are up in the 2018 elections so for them  if they may think their vote doesn't  necessarily matter so that could be why  people aren't showing up we've talked to  some women and they just feel like  they're kind of disillusioned from all  sides from Republicans Democrats they  kind of feel like no one's really has  our best interests in mind so why does  it even matter to vote yeah I you know I  was struck by the large number who said  that it didn't matter who controlled  Congress and one of the things I think  we'll be watching as these midterm  campaigns unfold is can the parties make  the argument that it does midterm  turnout as you pointed out and others is  often very low but it starts with that  idea that it matters you know when you  talk to folks and you you wouldn't you  cover politics from refinery29  are there other ways that younger people  are getting involved even if it's not  politics we're what are the channels for  their activism I think that social media  is a huge channel of activism for people  from sharing stories they care about  sharing statuses and then finding out  where protests and things are I think  people really like to show that they  care about issues on social media but  that doesn't necessarily translate into  real life effects like actually going to  the polls actually campaigning for a  candidate but I think people like to say  that they're involved and so if they  actually care  about issues but it's another thing to  actually you know register to vote and  go vote yeah and we saw in the polling  that a lot of these millennial women do  say that they post that they share that  they're engaged but that they recognize  it's not necessarily the most effective  thing it is though easy to do right and  this generation has been raised on  social media social media it's the  easiest way to share how you feel about  things and if you're like reading a news  story and you're really angry and fired  up you're gonna share that but then you  may go home kind of forget about it or  say like does it really matter if I vote  or not and the last affection I voted it  didn't go the way I wanted does it  really actually matter so I think that's  you're seeing that apathy for young  people everywhere so for the folks who  are going to vote and like we said there  are plenty of them you know it struck us  in the polling that they were very  motivated they were very enthusiastic  and that they cared about some  particular you know issues over others  one of them being health care yes so I  think for Otis across the board from  millennial to older women health care  was the most important part over things  like immigration and the economy and  civil rights I think because health care  is very personal  you can either afford to go to the  doctor or you can't or you're gonna end  up going into debt going to the doctor  you have an emergency and I think for  Millennials they see rising healthcare  cost everything is so expensive so they  that's really moving them because it  affects them very personally as compared  to other things that are a little bit  more abstract or in the background and  they see you can't really necessarily  change that quickly right you know  health care one of those issues the  economy as it affects their pocketbook  or this age group - it strikes me it  shows the most change in their lives for  any category that we ever we pollsters  put people in from 18 to 35 right you  might graduate from college if you go to  college you might get your first job buy  your first house you know start a family  all those things and yet it seemed like  the the economic part of it is it really  is all about whether they're affected  yeah and that starts to change over time  but we often see young people not quite  see that connection to politics  it's very immediate and that's wise  things like health care I'm a millennial  and for us like my parents generation  they could buy houses they didn't really  necessarily have to worry that much  about health care  they had more savings our generation  it's kind of we don't really have that  we are saddled with student loan debt so  as health care it affects your pockets  very personally we're really gonna care  about that since so many other things  are out of our control that's one thing  that we see like we really have to care  about yeah and many of them also came of  age after the recession during the the  recovery which didn't help everybody all  at once as well and you know one of the  other things that strikes you too is  this is the most diverse generation  compared to older Americans this  generation is more likely to know  someone or be from a family who are  immigrants right and and the composition  of this generation is the most diverse  it's it's just over  you know half white but also non-white  in large percentages how do you think  that is affecting all of these numbers  that we see today  I 