ne  of the other things we were talking  about earlier was the way in which as  people as voters react to the number of  women candidates who are running this  year which are in record numbers we  found the Democrats a little more likely  than Republicans to say that it would be  a good thing to have more women in  office to talk a little about that and  talk about how why you think that is and  why it is that voters told us that they  thought it would be good to have more  women in office so some people said that  they think it would be good for  democracy because it's a more diverse  pool of people who are in politics  Congress its majority male so if you  have more women elected it's going to be  more split other people said women are  better negotiators so things would  actually get done and then some people  said it it really doesn't matter but I  think the vast majority think that it's  time for it's not just to be a boys club  in Congress for women to finally take  the lead  Republicans were less likely to think  that it matters but then I think overall  people are like it wouldn't be a bad  thing for there'd be more diverse I  think not just gender but of thought in  Congress you know when you look at as  you cover the politics from and also  from the perspective of younger people  what is it that you think defines this  generation that makes them so different  or different from older generations and  what they're looking for and what they  want to hear I definitely think that we  are more connected than the older  generation so there's a lot more  exchange of ideas so we're hearing a lot  more things we're seeing a lot more  things than older people you're kind of  in your community you kind of thought  along the lines of people around you but  now we are so interconnected through  social media that we all are kind of  getting different ideas from all over  and I think that what that's what makes  us more different than any other  generation  and I think that activism online has  connected us but like I said it doesn't  really translate into politics for us  yeah well that sense of community it's  interesting you raise that because for a  long time voting studies have always  shown that once somebody feels like part  of a community they're more likely to  get engaged and more likely to vote and  interestingly enough it doesn't have to  be a physical community yeah now it can  be an online one or a digital one as we  go forward in the midterms  what are you looking for what do you  think is going to be pivotal not just  new controls Congress but in what kinds  of policies come out of this I think I  think healthcare is truly the number one  for me that's one thing I really really  care about and I think that I honestly  haven't heard that many candidates  really pushing home healthcare when  that's the most important thing I've  heard them talking about immigration  I've heard them talking about abortion  but health care has kind of been in the  background so if that's the main thing  that people care about I would like to  see how these different candidates are  going to address that we're almost two  months out so it's time for them to  start talking about that more great ok  everyone this has been the Toyota  greenroom live for with Ashley Edwards  of refinery29  i am anthony savant oh of CBS news with  our latest team up here on polling about  the midterm elections in millennial  women thank you very much for watching  have a good day
 

